WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:11.644
This is NOSHASHI: a desktop app for Mac, Windows and Linux that reads the live XRP Ledger, and tells an institution what it needs to know before it moves a token.

2
00:00:13.344 --> 00:00:17.867
Anyone holding a token on the XRP Ledger faces two questions.

3
00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:20.293
First: am I allowed to move this?

4
00:00:20.293 --> 00:00:28.919
That depends on what the issuer can still do, on credentials, on Travel Rule scope, on where the counterparty came from, and on your own policy.

5
00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:31.595
Second: could I actually get out of it?

6
00:00:31.595 --> 00:00:38.159
That depends on how much of the order book is really funded, on the pools, and on how concentrated the holders are.

7
00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:44.620
Compliance tools rarely read the order book, and market terminals rarely read the issuer's flags.

8
00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.019
NOSHASHI answers both from the same ledger reading, in the same second.

9
00:00:50.719 --> 00:00:53.774
Every adjudication ends in one of three verdicts.

10
00:00:53.774 --> 00:00:55.519
GO means every check passed.

11
00:00:55.969 --> 00:01:01.089
HOLD means a warning-level check did not pass, or something needs a person to review it.

12
00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:03.818
NO-GO means a blocking check failed.

13
00:01:03.818 --> 00:01:07.299
Do not move until it is resolved, or formally excepted.

14
00:01:07.749 --> 00:01:10.129
Each check reports one of five states.

15
00:01:10.129 --> 00:01:16.517
Insufficient data is an abstention, never a pass: NOSHASHI would rather say it cannot tell than guess.

16
00:01:18.217 --> 00:01:28.009
Every verdict produces a receipt: the ledger index it was read at, the rules version, every check's outcome, and a SHA-256 digest over all of it.

17
00:01:28.459 --> 00:01:38.443
Anyone who recomputes the digest later gets the same fingerprint, so a compliance file can prove what was known, and when, without anyone having to trust NOSHASHI.

18
00:01:40.143 --> 00:01:48.399
It is built for compliance officers screening assets and settlements, and for trading desks that need to know which positions they could really exit.

19
00:01:48.849 --> 00:01:52.847
Treasury teams use it to show auditors who controls the treasury.

20
00:01:52.847 --> 00:01:56.721
Token issuers use it to see their token the way holders see it.

21
00:01:57.171 --> 00:02:01.587
Auditors get evidence stamped with a ledger index that they can reproduce.

22
00:02:01.587 --> 00:02:05.107
And anyone can check an address before paying it, for free.

23
00:02:06.807 --> 00:02:12.823
You can start on the free plan: ten address checks a month, Ledger Sync, and the free tools on the website.

24
00:02:13.273 --> 00:02:17.223
NOSHASHI is in beta, at version one point zero point nine.

25
00:02:17.223 --> 00:02:25.327
It gives information, not financial or legal advice, and every reading carries its ledger index so you can re-check it.

26
00:02:25.777 --> 00:02:30.769
The full course, the knowledge check and the word list are at noshashi dot app slash learn.
